1
00:00:00,915 --> 00:00:09,959
Welcome to the South America capitals, episode two of three. First, I clear
the chosen names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,622 --> 00:00:20,897
Four capitals run down the Pacific and the interior: Peru on the desert coast,
the two Bolivian capitals in the highlands, and Paraguay on the river.

3
00:00:21,569 --> 00:00:23,612
Let's bring Lima into view.

4
00:00:27,858 --> 00:00:35,532
Lima sits on the Pacific side of Peru, on a plain of desert and fog about a
dozen kilometres inland from the sea.

5
00:00:36,189 --> 00:00:51,073
Francisco Pizarro founded it in 1535 as the City of Kings. It hardly ever
rains here, though a grey sea mist covers it for months, and its cathedral
holds the tomb of Pizarro himself.

6
00:00:51,730 --> 00:00:58,336
Remember Lima as the City of Kings under the mist, one of the driest cities on
earth.

7
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:01,381
Let's bring La Paz into view.

8
00:01:04,688 --> 00:01:16,495
La Paz lies in a canyon of the Bolivian Andes, about 3,650 metres above the
sea, with the city of El Alto on the plateau above it.

9
00:01:17,149 --> 00:01:28,875
Its cable-car network, Mi Teleférico, is the longest urban cable-car system in
the world, and its Witches' Market sells herbs and dried llama foetuses for
offerings.

10
00:01:29,525 --> 00:01:37,408
Remember La Paz as the canyon capital with the world's longest cable-car
network, and the witches' market under the snow.

11
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:40,428
Let's bring Sucre into view.

12
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:57,368
Sucre stands in the southern Bolivian Andes at about 2,800 metres, and it is
Bolivia's constitutional capital, while La Paz is the seat of government.

13
00:01:58,032 --> 00:02:10,490
Bolivia's independence was declared here in 1825; the city is named for
Antonio José de Sucre, and its white colonial centre is a UNESCO site.

14
00:02:11,141 --> 00:02:21,079
Remember Sucre as the city of four names — Charcas, La Plata, Chuquisaca and
Sucre — where Bolivia declared its independence.

15
00:02:21,766 --> 00:02:24,622
Let's bring Asunción into view.

16
00:02:28,040 --> 00:02:35,285
Asunción stands on the east bank of the Paraguay river, on a high terrace
above the lowlands of western Paraguay.

17
00:02:35,941 --> 00:02:51,719
Founded on the feast of the Assumption, 15 August 1537, it is one of the
oldest cities in South America, and from here the Spanish set out to found
Buenos Aires a second time, in 1580.

18
00:02:52,375 --> 00:03:02,058
Remember Asunción as the city founded on Assumption Day of 1537, from which
the Spanish sailed to rebuild Buenos Aires.

19
00:03:07,422 --> 00:03:17,616
Lima is the coastal city in the mist, La Paz and Sucre are the two Bolivian
capitals in the Andes, and Asunción is the old river town of the Gran Chaco.

20
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:30,507
Remember the City of Kings and Pizarro's tomb, the canyon of La Paz and its
witches' market, the four names of Sucre, and the founding of Asunción in
1537.
